.TH __CLRUCACHE_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__clrucache_destroy \- destroy an LRU cache (internal)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clrucache.h>

void __clrucache_destroy(clru_cache cache);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cache is an opaque VALUE handle (a packed {slot index, generation}
pair), resolved through a library-owned slot table before the
underlying cache object is ever touched. cache must be a currently
live handle (one returned by clrucache_create_full and not yet
destroyed). CLRU_CACHE_INVALID is a silent no-op. Any other value (a
stale handle already destroyed by an earlier call or concurrently
by another thread, a forged value, or garbage) is a FATAL ERROR:
this function calls fatal_err() (abort()/SIGABRT) rather than risk
a use-after-free or double-free.

On a live handle, waits for every in-flight resolved use of the
handle to finish (a clrucache_get_full/_set_full/clrucache_size/
_capacity call currently executing on another thread, including one
still blocked inside a slow remote getter/setter callback), then
evicts all remaining live entries (invoking the eviction callback
for each while the mutex is held), destroys the internal hash map,
frees the mutex and the handle's own pin condition variable, and
releases all memory.

This is the low-level destructor. In application code use the
clru_destroy() macro, which also sets the handle to
CLRU_CACHE_INVALID.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cache  Cache handle to destroy. Passing CLRU_CACHE_INVALID is a
       no-op.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_destroy (3),
.BR clrucache_create_full (3)
